Let \(F\) be a finite CNF formula and \(C\in F\). Starting with \(D:=C\), repeatedly apply hidden-literal addition: if \(l_0\in D\), \((l_0\lor l)\in F\), and neither \(l\) nor \(
eg l\) belongs to \(D\), set \(D:=D\cup\{
eg l\}\). Delete \(C\) if the resulting closure \(D\) is tautological, or if some \(u\in D\) blocks \(D\), meaning every resolvent \((D\setminus\{u\})\cup(E\setminus\{
eg u\})\) with \(E\in F\) and \(
eg u\in E\) is tautological. Repeating such deletions to a fixed point produces \(F'\) with \(F\) satisfiable if and only if \(F'\) is satisfiable.

For finite CNF \(F\), clause \(C\in F\), and literal \(l\in C\), define the resolution candidates \(\operatorname{RC}(F,C,l)\) as clauses \(E\in F\) containing \(
eg l\) for which \((C\setminus\{l\})\cup(E\setminus\{
eg l\})\) is not tautological. When this set is nonempty, define \(\operatorname{RI}(F,C,l):= igcap_{E\in\operatorname{RC}(F,C,l)}(E\setminus\{
eg l\})\). A covered-literal extension step replaces \(C\) by \(C\cup\operatorname{RI}(F,C,l)\) whenever the intersection adds a literal. Iterating all applicable steps to a fixed point yields \(\operatorname{CLA}(F,C)\), which is satisfiability-equivalent to replacing \(C\) by its closure in \(F\).

The clause \(C\) has the resolution asymmetric tautology property on pivot \(p\) with respect to \(F\) if, for every \(D\in F\) containing \(
eg p\), the resolvent \(R=(C\setminus\{p\})\cup(D\setminus\{
eg p\})\) is tautological or satisfies \(F\land igwedge_{\ell\in R}
eg\ell\vdash_1 ot\). A RAT addition may therefore introduce a clause not entailed by \(F\), but \(F\) and \(F\land C\) are equisatisfiable. A RAT refutation ends by validly adding \(\varnothing\).
